l to $2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269176 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc5eba78-8576-573c-99ab-7374b3ba103b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269176 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186269479 #edb/ref 1080 "7-4\\text{i}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269479 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269479 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "192a18d2-9632-54a2-8edf-f7f1746b01ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269805 #edb/ref 1080 "8+7\\text{i}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269805 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175921860454